  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Tue Sep 2 2026\n\nOvernight: Strong to Severe Storms\n\nAs the 30 to 40 knot 850 mb nocturnal jet gradually veers from \nsouthwest to west overnight, the a severe thunderstorm complex \nwill gradually turn and move along the Interstate 90 corridor. \nAs the cold pool continues to coalesce, this complex will become\na progressive linear mesoscale convective complex. The biggest \nquestion at this time will be when will this eastward turn \noccurs. If this occurs too late (between 2 and 4 am), this \nsystem will have less downdraft CAPE and more convective \ninhibition (CIN) to contend with as it moves further east. In \nthis scenario, the winds would remain aloft and the system would\nrapidly weaken as it moves away from the better instability. If\nthis system turns east by 2 am, the CIN would be less than 50 \nJ/kg and the downdraft CAPE would be around 1000 J/kg. Under \nthis scenario, there will be a possibility for narrow band of \nstrong to severe winds (40-65 mph) on the southern edge of \nbowing complex. The wind threat would be 1 to 2 counties wide in\nsoutheast Minnesota and along and north of I-90 in western \nWisconsin. There might also be a brief QLCS tornado threat near \nthe Minnesota and Iowa corridor. At this time, scenario looks \nmore likely.\n\nFurther east, there is a quasi-stationary boundary stationed \nalong the I-90 corridor. Storm parameters of MLCAPE ahead of the\ntrough are between 1500 and 2500 J/kg with sufficient deep \nlayer shear (0-6km) of 45 to 55 knots. The expectation is that \nthese storms will grow upscale and merge into the MCC. Soundings\nsuggest that these elevated storms will occasionally split with\nthe left movers being favored to produce large (hail up to golf\nball) as they move northeast at 40 to 45 knots.\n\nToday: Scattered storms continue\n\nThe quasi-stationary boundary will remain near and south of the\nI-90 corridor. With 0-1 km most-unstable CAPES of 2500 to 3500 \nJ/kg and 30-40 knot 0-6 km shear, there will be the possibility \nof scattered strong to severe storms. \n\nHigh temperatures will range from the upper 70s to mid-80s north\nof Interstate 94 and from the mid-80s to lower 90s elsewhere.\n\nTonight and Thursday morning: Another thunderstorm complex\n\nAnother mesoscale convective complex will develop across\nnorthwest Minnesota this evening on a nose of a 25-30 knot \n850 mb jet. As this nocturnal jet gradually veers overnight, \nthis complex is expected to shift east and then southeast. With\nweakening 0-6 km shear overnight, not expecting any severe\norganized severe weather.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the upper 50s to mid-60s north\nof Interstate 94 and from the mid-60s to lower 70s.\n\nThursday night into Friday Morning: Another thunderstorm complex\n\nShowers and storms will develop ahead of a shortwave trough late\nThursday afternoon and evening over northeast South Dakota and\ncentral and northwest Minnesota. As these cold pools coalesce,\na thunderstorm complex will gradually develop. Over time as the\nlow level jet veers and the cold pool grows large, this will\nlikely become a rather progressive linear mesoscale convective \ncomplex which will move southeast through the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley. The main severe weather threat will be damaging \nwinds.",
